joining us now is the German ambassador  to the UK Miguel Berger thank you so  much for being with us this morning  um it's been a bumpy few months for the  UK uh shall we say you know partly  because of global factors like inflation  uh partly because of domestic issues as  well and what do you think the UK is  currently perceived from abroad  yeah I would say that everybody is glad  to see that financial markets are now  again more stable but the challenges you  have to described we are all facing them  high inflation Energy prices going up  governments struggling to get the  necessary support for business and  private households so I would say there  is also quite some understanding for the  challenges yeah those challenges as you  say um experienced by countries across  Europe the rest of the world as well and  what do you see as the you know Way  Forward for that  I think we have to be aware that this  energy crisis will not go away after  winter because we will have zero Russian  gas in Europe and Russia has been by far  the largest supplier that means that not  only Germany the whole of Europe UK will  have to get cash from the international  markets and that means for the next one  and a half two years we might face a  situation with high energy prices let's  talk a bit about Ukraine  um shall we because I think the German  Chancellor spoke to Vladimir Putin this  week on the phone uh he urged him to  withdraw troops and to try and find a  diplomatic solution and it's Germany  optimistic about a way out of the wall  we are not yet optimistic we don't see a  fundamental change in the Russian  position  Putin continues this really terrorizing  the civilian population we are very  concerned about that because these  attacks on the energy infrastructure  mean that many people with freezing  temperatures might be forced to leave  Ukraine so we expect another wave of  refugees in the coming weeks so but to  your question no we don't see for the  foreseeable future a willingness to  engage in the Diplomatic solution in the  conversation Allah Schultz also vowed to  continue German support for Ukraine and  there has been some criticism hasn't  there that Germany was a bit slow of the  mark in offering support to Ukraine  perhaps you could have done a little bit  more as some would say in terms of  Military Support as well I think we can  show that you know the different amounts  that countries have spent on weapons and  equipment to Ukraine it's the numbers  just on the left there you can see the  us at the top followed by the EU but  both Poland and the UK above Germany  despite having smaller populations  should Germany do more  I think these figures are not correct  because we have sent many weapon systems  which were written off  so for example the anti-aerial a gay  part tanks which were already been  written off so we can't have them in  this statistic as new tanks so I would  not give too much on it but my feeling  is that we start at the middle a little  bit later than others but in the end if  you look at the amount of weapon systems  especially  Iris T anti-aerial missiles they are  extremely effective 100 of Russian  missiles attacking Kiev the last raid  were shot down so I would say we are  doing all quite well and I guess there's  been action on energy as well in the eu2  where there is obviously more of a  dependence on Russian oil and gas yeah  but this has been dramatically reduced  but let me say on weapons I think the  key issue is that we continue to send  weapons and ammunition and assure  maintenance of the equipment we have  sent over the coming months only if that  happens Russia will understand that they  will not really be able to to defeat  your Ukraine so this is I think the key  challenge for all of us  um before we move on to brexit which I  do want to do um I just want to ask you  a question on China because we've seen  some really extraordinary protests in  China recently  um originally about the lockdown but  also spilling over to  um other issues as well um Chancellor  Schultz announced recently that Germany  is going to try and reduce its  dependence on trade with China that will  be quite difficult to do though won't it  it's a challenge for all of us and I  think the key issue is that we have to  diversify our trade relations so we have  to look at our weaknesses the weaknesses  like for example semiconductors  batteries Rare Earth so we have to  invest in Western production try to  re-shore certain industries but let me  say that we are working in Germany on a  new China strategy and we are  cooperating whether very closely with  the British government which are which  is doing the update of the integrated  review so I would say we are analyzing  together the challenges of China it's  interesting that the UK and Germany you  see is perhaps working together to get a  new sort of strategy on this then yes  and we have to do that I think if there  is one lesson of Russia's war of  aggression is that we have to reduce  dependencies from China  um now there's been lots of speculation  in the UK but Rishi sunx approach to  brexit  um are you optimistic that there could  be some kind of breakthrough uh in the  disagreement about the Northern Ireland  protocol I think that we have now a  different basis for negotiations I see  an openness to engage there have already  been talks and yes I would say I'm  cautiously optimistic because in the end  there is a lending zone for the Northern  Ireland protocols implementation it is  possible to find a solution the  difficult issue is is more the political  on the reaction of the dup the risk of a  of an election in Northern Ireland so  there are many political issues which go  together with it but if we speak about  the negotiation process I would say I'm  cautiously optimistic that's interesting  do you think then that Rishi Cena has a  different attitude a different set of  priorities perhaps to list trust and  perhaps also boss Johnson  yeah I I would say that even less trust  made some steps towards Europe she  participated in Prague at the first  meeting of the European political  community and the first talks were  already under this trust with James  cleverly so um I would say we see in  general a different attitude maybe it  has also to do with the war in Ukraine  that everybody understands that in the  west we have more important challenges  to face and that democracy should work  together and should be able to sort out  an issue like Northern Ireland with the  um you know you talk about the the  threat from Ukraine and Russia but also  obviously we are facing an economic  crisis as well right with energy bills  and given that do you think that there  is a desire both from the EU side but  also from the British side I guess to  have a closer relationship I would say  yes and it has always been the position  of the German government that we want a  relationship as close as possible with  the United Kingdom and yes you mentioned  energy this is a perfect example if we  look at the challenges electricity  supply for the UK in Winter a lot of LNG  went through the UK to Germany to the  center of Europe so energy is a perfect  example of of an area where we should  cooperate to Mutual Advantage there's  been speculation about so-called Swiss  steel which effectively means you know  access to the single Market it would  come with greater contributions to the  EU budget and also more immigration from  Europe as well officially the UK  government has ruled out but I guess  what I'm interested in is would that  even be a possibility it would even be  something that would be on the table  from the European side  I would say that we welcome that there  is a discussion about this kind of we  don't have to go into the details of the  Swiss model but that the government or  parts of the government are thinking  about how to have a better and closer  relationship with the European Union is  something that that we see very  positively  it feels that kirstan is perhaps  reluctant to talk too much about brexit  he of course was a very strong remainer  and would you like to see a stronger  line from labor  I think that's really the decision that  labor has to take but we see also very  positively that labor is willing to have  a very close relationship with Europe  and I think the rest we will have to  leave it now to the negotiations because  if we achieve a solution on Northern  Ireland then this will help also to  develop other areas of cooperation so I  would see the solution of the Northern  Ireland protocol question as a first  step and there will be others then to  follow  um there's a lot to talk about in  independent Scotland here in the UK as  well if Scotland was to vote for  Independence and would they have to  adopt the euro to join the EU  you know I don't really want to get into  okay internal question but let me put it  like that every country that is joining  uh the European Union and we are  currently negotiating with some  countries in the western Balkans know  that they have to introduce the Euro  um interesting um and then just finally  could you see a position in the future  where the UK joins the single Market  very difficult to predict I would say  let's take it step by step  um but in Germany obviously there is a a  lot of interest in having as I said the  United Kingdom as close as possible so  Europe and to Germany uh thank you um  now I'm sorry to bring itself but the  World Cup is of course still going on uh  Germany are out thanks to a winning goal  from Japan against Spain that knocked  you out and there was a pretty  controversial VAR decision uh in the  lead up to that goal that this it was  ruled that this ball didn't go out of  play  do you agree just look at this picture  that the ball didn't cross the line  yeah it seems that the ball was in maybe  three four millimeters but you know in  the end this doesn't matter because  Germany should never have lost the match  the first match against Japan so let me  only say really good luck for the  english team tonight against Senegal um  oh there you go you're going to be  supporting England against  that yeah it will be an interesting  match to watch  um and just finally I just want to show  you uh this picture of the German team  because they were certainly a team who  tried to make their voices heard in  Qatar covering their mouths there to  send a very clear message that they  weren't allowed to I guess show their  um disagreements with some of the  qataris positions on human rights  um were you proud of the German team for  doing this and would you have liked to  see more coordinate in action from  European teams on this yeah let me say I  was really proud of seeing seeing them  because it was a very controversial  discussion about the World Cup in Qatar  as it was in the United Kingdom and  especially this very appalling interview  by the head of FIFA Mr Infantino so I  think if there is one conclusion out of  that is that we should only have this  big sporting events in countries which  respect workers rights and respect basic  human rights that's interesting to hear  you say that so uh family I think many  people would agree with that analysis as  well at uh thank you so much for being  on the program this morning good to have  you here thank you thank you

